# Camera movement
@scenario tutorial_lesson_13
@include /definitions/characters.vns
@label start
[mode dialogue standard]
[set ui.dialogueUi project]
[bg room]
[char ginger global on]
[set ui.dialogueFadeMs 160]
[stage clear]
[show ginger beside_code thoughtful]
[set tutorial_source scripts/tutorial/13_camera_and_staging.vns]
[set tutorial_excerpt framing]
[set ui.dialogueFacet tutorial_code]
ginger: This is a close-up made with a sprite transform. Watch the size of the face and its position against the edge of the frame.
[if !tutorial_still]
# region framing
timeline {
  parallel {
    move "ginger" { x:-48 y:24 dur:640 easing:ease_out }
    scale "ginger" { sx:1.16 sy:1.16 dur:640 easing:ease_out }
  }
}
# endregion
[endif]
ginger: The target in both tracks is {b}ginger{/b}. Scale makes the sprite larger. The X and Y offsets adjust where the enlarged sprite lands.
ginger: Keep the scale at 1.16. In Puppeteer, change only the horizontal offset and check where the eyes land relative to the centre.
ginger: Now set the offset to zero. Compare the space above the head and the overlap with the dialogue box at the same window size.
ginger: Scaling changes the distance between features on the sprite. Moving shifts those features together. Pick two landmarks, such as the eyes and collar, and track both against the frame.
ginger: The example pairs a 1.16 scale with an offset. The scale enlarges the face. The offset brings the enlarged drawing into the intended part of the frame.
[clear ui.dialogueFacet]
> Frame the eyes at two scales -> practice
> Review question -> check
> Back to topics -> return_hub
@label check
ginger: Why pair an offset with a scale change?
> To adjust the subject’s framing as it grows. -> correct
> To change the speaker name. -> retry
> Back to topics -> return_hub
@label retry
[show ginger guide confused]
ginger: Scaling changes how much of the frame the sprite occupies. Consider where the important detail will land.
[jump check]
@label correct
[show ginger guide normal]
ginger: The offset adjusts the framing as the sprite grows. You can change the composition without replacing the image.
> Back to topics -> return_hub
> Replay example -> start
@label return_hub
[clear ui.dialogueFacet]
[if !tutorial_done_13]
[flag tutorial_done_13]
[inc tutorial_count]
[endif]
[stage clear]
[goto TutorialHub:topics_page_2]
[end]

@label practice
[show ginger beside_code thoughtful]
[set tutorial_source scripts/tutorial/13_camera_and_staging.vns]
[set tutorial_excerpt framing]
[set ui.dialogueFacet tutorial_code]
ginger: Take a screenshot before the timeline and mark the eye line and the top of the dialogue box. Those references make small position changes easier to compare.
ginger: Set sx and sy to 1.0 while keeping the offset. Look for a translation with no change in the distance between the eyes and collar.
ginger: Restore 1.16 on both axes and zero the offset. The distance between those landmarks should grow. Check whether the eyes still land at the height you wanted.
ginger: Finally restore the offset and compare all three versions at the same window size. If a face is framed well but the name tag covers the hands, inspect the lower part of the shot too.
@label practice_question
ginger: The face is the right size, but the eyes sit too far left. Which parameter should you change first?
> The horizontal position offset. -> practice_result
> Both scale targets. -> practice_hint
> Back to topics -> return_hub
@label practice_hint
ginger: Scaling changes the size you already approved. First move the existing framing horizontally.
[jump practice_question]
@label practice_result
ginger: A horizontal shift moves both landmarks together and preserves their separation. Check the eye line again after the change.
[clear ui.dialogueFacet]
> Review question -> check
> Try the exercise again -> practice
> Back to topics -> return_hub
